  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Execution / Implant Launch", "technique": "T1059 + T1204", "what_happened": "At 2028-06-26 02:18:42 CEST, Sysmon Event 1 on WS-CLOUD-04.corp.local (10.30.7.62) recorded process creation for image=C:\\Users\\hassan.b\\AppData\\Roaming\\icmpshell.exe under user CORP\\hassan.b with parentImage=explorer.exe. The binary is UNSIGNED (signed=false) and runs from a per-user roaming AppData path — a classic LOLBin-adjacent stage-2 implant location. The name 'icmpshell' literally signals ICMP-based shell, a known covert-C2 family. parent=explorer.exe indicates an interactive double-click launch (the user opened the file from desktop / file explorer), not a system service or scheduled task.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1 (rule.id 61603, level 8). Linkage by image path + user + agent.name. The combination unsigned + AppData\\Roaming + parent=explorer.exe + interactive-launch is a Stage-2-implant fingerprint.", "first_seen": "2028-06-26T02:18:42+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Command & Control / Application Layer Protocol", "technique": "T1095", "what_happened": "At 02:22:14 CEST (3 minutes 32 seconds after implant launch), Suricata logged ICMP_ECHO from src=10.30.7.62 to dest=91.219.236.87 with packet_size=512 bytes. The Suricata rule tagged is 'ET POLICY ICMP Large Packet'. Normal Windows ping.exe emits a FIXED 32-byte payload by default; 512 bytes in a single ICMP echo is ~16x larger and indicates the Data field is being used to carry application data (covert tunneling per MITRE T1095 — Non-Application Layer Protocol). The 3-min 32-sec gap between implant launch and first beacon is consistent with an implant doing initial host fingerprinting / sleep-jitter before its first call-out.", "chain_reason": "Suricata data.alert.signature='ET POLICY ICMP Large Packet' on data.src_ip=10.30.7.62 → data.dest_ip=91.219.236.87. The 512-byte payload size is a hard anomaly indicator vs the 32-byte normal-ping baseline.", "first_seen": "2028-06-26T02:22:14+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Command & Control / Non-Application Process Tunneling", "technique": "T1095", "what_happened": "At 02:22:18 CEST (4 seconds after the Suricata beacon detection), Sysmon Event 3 (Network connection) on WS-CLOUD-04 recorded protocol=ICMP with image=C:\\Users\\hassan.b\\AppData\\Roaming\\icmpshell.exe and dest_ip=91.219.236.87. This is the endpoint-side confirmation of step 2: the process emitting ICMP packets is icmpshell.exe — NOT the standard Windows ping.exe (C:\\Windows\\System32\\ping.exe) or tracert.exe. On a normal workstation, the ONLY processes emitting outbound ICMP are ping.exe, tracert.exe, pathping.exe, and a small set of management tools — any user-AppData process emitting ICMP is highly atypical.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 3 with data.win.eventdata.image=user-AppData path + protocol=ICMP. Cross-references with Suricata step 2 by timestamp + same src→dest pair + same protocol — endpoint + network views of the same beacon flow.", "first_seen": "2028-06-26T02:22:18+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Command & Control / Beacon Pattern Continuation", "technique": "T1095", "what_happened": "At 02:22:42 CEST (28 seconds after step 2), Suricata logged a second ICMP_ECHO from 10.30.7.62 → 91.219.236.87, packet_size=512 bytes again. The pattern continues every ~30 seconds for the remainder of the observation window. Sustained, periodic, same-size ICMP echoes between two endpoints is a textbook covert-tunnel beaconing signature: the implant is calling out for tasking, the C2 is responding via ICMP_REPLY with command payloads. Legitimate ping bursts are short (typically 4 packets then exit on Windows); legitimate monitoring pings are usually very small (32 bytes) — 512-byte sustained periodic ICMP is operationally indistinguishable from a C2 tunnel.", "chain_reason": "Repeated Suricata ICMP_ECHO with identical src→dest + identical packet_size=512 + ~30s inter-packet interval. The third+ beacon confirms 'pattern' rather than 'one-off oddity'.", "first_seen": "2028-06-26T02:22:42+0200"},
    {"step": 5, "tactic": "Detection / Custom Decoder Correlation", "technique": "Custom — ICMP_ANOMALY rule", "what_happened": "At 02:24:18 CEST, a Wazuh custom decoder fired ALERT 'ICMP_ANOMALY — sustained ICMP traffic 512-byte payload >5 minutes, single src→single dest, src=non-system process (not standard ping)'. The decoder correlates multiple data points: (a) sustained traffic duration, (b) atypically large payload, (c) point-to-point flow without diversification, (d) source process category. Receiving this alert means the SIEM-side detection logic for 'covert ICMP C2' fired — the chain is now an analyst-actionable detection, not just a sequence of low-level signals.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh custom rule firing at level 12+ correlating Sysmon Event 3 + Suricata ICMP_ECHO bursts on the same (src_ip, dest_ip, process_image) tuple over a >5-min window.", "first_seen": "2028-06-26T02:24:18+0200"},
    {"step": 6, "tactic": "Execution / Post-C2 Discovery", "technique": "T1059.003 + T1033 + T1087.001", "what_happened": "At 02:27:42 CEST, Sysmon Event 1 on WS-CLOUD-04 recorded image=C:\\Windows\\System32\\cmd.exe with commandLine='cmd.exe /c whoami /priv' and parentImage=C:\\Users\\hassan.b\\AppData\\Roaming\\icmpshell.exe under user CORP\\hassan.b. This is the smoking gun confirming the ICMP tunnel is functional: a command sent FROM the external C2 (91.219.236.87) THROUGH the ICMP tunnel was DECODED and EXECUTED on the endpoint. whoami /priv enumerates the current user's Windows privileges (SeDebugPrivilege, SeImpersonatePrivilege, etc.) — the standard post-C2-establishment recon step before privilege escalation attempts.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1 with parentImage=icmpshell.exe + commandLine='cmd.exe /c whoami /priv'. The parent-child relationship icmpshell.exe→cmd.exe→whoami.exe is a DIRECT demonstration that the implant is receiving and executing commands. Without ICMP tunneling working, this exec would not happen.", "first_seen": "2028-06-26T02:27:42+0200"}
  ],
